          "settings": {
            "question": "Which UK criminal record check do I need to teach abroad?",
            "answer": "<p>Visas usually need an ACRO police certificate. GOV.UK says an employer abroad can't request a DBS check, and teachers working with children overseas can apply for an International Child Protection Certificate (ICPC).</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long does a South African police clearance take?",
            "answer": "<p>SAPS says about 15 working days from when its Criminal Record Centre in Pretoria receives your application, and the fee is R114. You apply at a police station, or at a South African embassy if you're abroad. DIRCO treats the certificate as valid for six months, so time it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need an apostille or legalisation?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on whether your destination is part of the Hague Apostille Convention. Canada and Saudi Arabia accept apostilles; Qatar, the UAE and Kuwait don't, so documents go through their embassy and foreign ministry.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Who apostilles South African documents?",
            "answer": "<p>DIRCO, free of charge, for original public documents. High Courts handle notarised documents. Degrees need a SAQA verification letter first and matric an Umalusi one, and DIRCO doesn't accept certified copies.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I open a bank account before I arrive in the Gulf?",
            "answer": "<p>Usually not. Banks ask for your residence ID: HSBC Qatar asks for a Qatar ID and opens accounts in a branch, and UAE banks list an Emirates ID and residence visa. Bring enough money to cover the weeks until then.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will my employer give me health insurance?",
            "answer": "<p>In the UAE, yes: employers must provide it in Dubai, Abu Dhabi and, since January 2025, the other emirates. In Qatar, the law puts the duty on employers, so ask your school how it applies to you and your family.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I bring my dog?",
            "answer": "<p>Usually, but the rules differ by country and take months to meet. Qatar requires an import permit from the Ministry of Municipality, and Canada requires a valid rabies vaccination certificate for dogs aged 8 months or older from countries that aren't rabies-free, such as South Africa.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I still pay South African tax while teaching abroad?",
            "answer": "<p>If you remain an SA tax resident, the first R1.25 million of foreign employment income can be exempt when you meet SARS's 183-day and 60-day tests. Financial emigration on its own doesn't end tax residency, so get advice from a registered tax practitioner.</p>"
          }
        }
